from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3401';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2: transducers, weighted specifications and approximate synthesis; Section 4, Lemma 10 and Theorems 12–13.'
refs += [dict(id='full2021',title='Synthesis from Weighted Specifications with Partial Domains over Finite Words — full version',authors='Emmanuel Filiot; Christof Löding; Sarah Winter',year=2021,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.05550',locator='Version 1, 9 March 2021; Section 2, Section 4 before Theorem 13, and Appendix C.4.4, Theorem 27.'),dict(id='determinization2024',title='Determinization of Integral Discounted-Sum Automata is Decidable',authors='Shaull Almagor; Neta Dafni',year=2024,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-57228-9_10',locator='Abstract and introduction: functional determinization with integral reciprocal discount, including non-complete automata.')]
save(i,dict(title='Decidability of approximate synthesis with arbitrary rational discounting',formal='Is approximate synthesis decidable for deterministic discounted-sum specifications over finite words, with an arbitrary rational discount factor 0<λ<1? Given a finite deterministic weighted automaton defining S, a rational tolerance ν and a comparison symbol ⋖∈{<,≤}, decide whether there exists a deterministic finite-state transducer T such that dom(T)=dom(S) and, for every input word u in this domain, S(u⊗T(u)) is finite and bestVal_S(u)−S(u⊗T(u)) ⋖ ν. Here bestVal_S(u)=max{S(u⊗v): v has length |u| and S(u⊗v)>−∞}. One algorithm must halt and answer correctly on every such finite input, including both comparison modes; its input includes λ. The source settles the subclass λ=1/k for integers k≥2 and leaves general rational λ open.',definitions='Let I and O be finite nonempty, disjoint input and output alphabets. For u=a₁…a_n∈I* and v=b₁…b_n∈O*, their convolution here is the alternating word u⊗v=a₁b₁…a_nb_n, of length 2n. A deterministic weighted automaton consists of finitely many states, one initial state, a partial transition function on I∪O, a set of accepting states and one integer weight on each transition. If the unique run on u⊗v exists and ends in an accepting state, and its transition weights are z₁,…,z_{2n}, then S(u⊗v)=Σ_{j=1}^{2n}λʲz_j. Otherwise S(u⊗v)=−∞. An accepting empty run has value zero. Discounting is per individual input or output transition, begins with λ¹, and has no multiplying normalization factor 1−λ. The domain dom(S) consists of the input words admitting at least one finite-valued output word. The maximum defining bestVal is attained because there are finitely many length-|u| output words. T has finite states, an initial state, a total deterministic transition map (state,input symbol)↦(output symbol,next state), and accepting states. It outputs exactly one symbol immediately after each input symbol, with no lookahead or end-of-word output; T(u) is defined precisely when the run ends in an accepting state. Its acceptance domain must equal dom(S), even though its internal run can produce symbols on other inputs. The number of transducer states is not bounded in advance. Thus a single finite-state policy must work for all word lengths and all input continuations, while the offline comparison bestVal may use the entire word. All transitions, alphabets, weights, λ and ν have finite explicit encodings; integers are binary and rationals have binary numerators and positive denominators. No computation-time bound, error probability or approximation of the decision answer is permitted. The tolerance is an additive difference in discounted value, not a ratio or per-symbol average.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Give a total decision algorithm for existence of a finite-state realizer satisfying the exact domain and additive regret condition, or prove this decision problem undecidable. Report which comparison modes are covered. Deciding only fixed-threshold synthesis S(u⊗T(u))≥c, only zero regret, only reciprocal-integer discounting, or only a prescribed finite horizon does not decide the stated general problem. A procedure that finds a realizer on yes instances but may run forever on no instances is not a decider.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Online outputs are compared with hindsight',text='For each completed input word, the specification assigns values to admissible output words. Approximate synthesis asks for one finite-state online transducer whose value stays within a given additive tolerance of the best output chosen with full knowledge of that word.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Partial domains are part of correctness',text='Some input words have no admissible output. The synthesized transducer must recognize exactly the specification’s input domain and meet the quantitative requirement on every word in it. The domain need not be prefix closed, so accepted finite executions cannot be replaced by a requirement on all prefixes indiscriminately.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The already solved tasks are different',text='The source proves decidability for fixed-threshold and best-value discounted-sum synthesis. For approximate synthesis it gives NEXPTIME and EXPTIME upper bounds for strict and non-strict comparison respectively when λ=1/k. The March 2021 full version repeats the general-rational open question and contains the detailed special-case result.',citation='full2021'),dict(title='Related later determinization does not cover this target',text='Almagor and Dafni establish decidability of functional determinization for discounted-sum automata with an integral divisor greater than one, including automata outside the complete setting. This corresponds to reciprocal-integer discounting in the present convention. Functional determinization is also a different decision problem from synthesizing an online transducer with bounded regret; their result does not settle arbitrary rational λ here.',citation='determinization2024')],why='Would determine whether near-optimal terminating reactive behavior can be synthesized when future rewards are discounted by any rational factor, extending beyond arithmetic restrictions on the discount and beyond exact-optimum synthesis.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='The source establishes the reciprocal-integer-discount subclass and leaves approximate synthesis for general rational discounting open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2021-03-09',text='The full version preserves the question and supplies the detailed proof of the special-case upper bounds.',citation='full2021'),dict(date='2024',text='Functional determinization is proved decidable for integral-divisor discounted-sum automata; it is a related restricted-discount result, not a decision procedure for this general synthesis task.',citation='determinization2024')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the deterministic finite-word model, partial domain, transducer convention, regret condition and exact discount indexing in the conference and full versions. Checked later discounted-sum determinization/comparison work and focused synthesis searches through 12 September 2026. No result settling the general rational-discount question was found; searches are not exhaustive and theorem proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A decidability boundary for quantitative reactive synthesis with hindsight comparison and arbitrary rational discounting. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Restored approximate synthesis as the missing antecedent and corrected the damaged reciprocal-integer condition. Defined exact partial-domain realization, finite-state online output, additive tolerance and discounting on both input and output transitions; separated the known zero-regret and threshold cases.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input specification is a deterministic weighted automaton on alternating input and output symbols.','A finite-state transducer must generate outputs online and accept exactly the input words for which some admissible output exists.','Its discounted value must stay within a given additive tolerance of the best admissible output chosen in hindsight for the same finite input word.','The source decides this for reciprocal-integer discounts but leaves arbitrary rational discounts open.','The later functional-determinization result concerns the restricted discount regime and does not settle the general approximate-synthesis question.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full2021','determinization2024'])),notes=['Recovered approximate synthesis, distinct from already decidable best-value and threshold synthesis.','Defined alternating transition-level discounting, exact domain, accepting transducer and offline comparator.','Specified rational encoding, both strictness modes, unbounded horizon and unrestricted finite memory.','Restored λ=1/k and checked full version plus later restricted-discount determinization.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-30829-1_18'])
